message.content:
{
  "summary": "This bill would require every local education agency (LEA) and public charter school to designate one instructional day each school year to observe the Fourth of July. Students in grades K-12 would receive age- and grade-appropriate instruction on the founding of the United States, the Declaration of Independence, the U.S. Constitution, and American civic principles. 

The bill sets specific learning objectives based on grade level. Students in grades K-5 would be introduced to basic concepts of independence and national symbols. Students in grades 6-8 would focus on the historical context of independence and civic principles, potentially through the social studies curriculum. Students in grades 9-12 would analyze foundational texts and civic themes, potentially through the English language arts curriculum. 

Directors of schools for LEAs and public charter schools would be responsible for designating the day, ensuring consistent implementation across their schools, and verifying that the instruction aligns with state academic standards. The bill requires that the instruction be nonpartisan, educational, and focused on historical facts and founding documents. LEAs and charter schools must also periodically review and update their instructional materials. The bill would take effect upon becoming law and apply starting with the 2026-2027 school year."
}
